mapsearch -- search .map sources for entities
=============================================

mapsearch takes three regular expressions -- a classname, a property name and
a value -- and prints every entity in every map that matches all three, with
the file, the line, the coordinates, and flag values broken out into boxes.


Credit
------
MapSearch is Inky's tool, and thanks are due to him for it. Every idea here is
his: the three-argument search, matching an integer against the bits of
spawnflags, the .pts point files, the shape of the config. It carries his name
at his request, and with his permission.

    http://earthday.free.fr/Inkys-Hexen-II-Mapping-Corner/

See his site for the full documentation and a Windows build. His is the
original and the one he maintains; PimpModel and the rest of his mapping
documentation live there too.

This build shares none of his code. It exists so the uHexen2 toolchain has an
entity search that compiles from source next to qbsp and light on every
platform we ship, including headless Linux.


Command line
------------
    mapsearch CLASSNAME [PROPERTY] [VALUE] [OPTIONS]

Only CLASSNAME is required. All three are regular expressions. Leave one out,
or pass "*" or ".*", to accept anything.

  On a Unix shell, quote the "*": write 'mapsearch "*" spawnflags 64', not
  'mapsearch * spawnflags 64', or the shell expands it to file names.

A pattern must match the WHOLE name or value. "func_door" does not find
func_door_rotating; "func_door.*" does. A sound reference is ".*\.wav".

Supported syntax:

    .              any character
    [abc] [^a-z]   character classes, with ranges and negation
    \d \D          a digit / anything else
    \w \W          a word character ([A-Za-z0-9_]) / anything else
    \s \S          whitespace / anything else
    \. \\ \* ...   any punctuation character, taken literally
    \t \n \r       tab, newline, carriage return
    (...)          grouping; (?:...) is accepted and means the same
    a|b            alternation
    ^ $            start and end of the value
    * + ?          zero or more, one or more, zero or one
    {n} {n,} {n,m} counted repetition
    *? +? ?? {}?   the lazy forms of all of the above

Case is ignored by default; turn that off with the "i" option or in the config
file.

An integer VALUE matches literally and as a set of checked boxes in the flag
properties (see FlagsProperties below). "mapsearch "*" spawnflags 2" finds
every entity with AT LEAST the 2 box checked. To match one exact value only,
post-filter the output or remove spawnflags from FlagsProperties in the
config.


[OPTIONS]
---------
A run of the letters below, each switched on by a preceding "+" (assumed if
you write neither) or off by a "-". A letter you leave out keeps whatever the
config file says.

    l   also write the results to a log file
    n   sort by line number, instead of classname then targetname then
        property name
    p   write a .pts point file for each map with results, named after the
        map and beside it. Open it in TrenchBroom to step through the hits.
    i   ignore case (on unless the config says otherwise)
    d   developer mode: report on stderr what is being read and skipped

    +l-p    write a log, no point files, default sort order
    lnp     switch on l, n and p

A bare run of letters like "lnp" is only read as options once CLASSNAME,
PROPERTY and VALUE have all been given. Anything starting with "+" or "-" is
read as options wherever it appears, except a negative number, so searching
for -1 works.

    --config=FILE   read this config file instead of looking for one
    --help          the built-in usage summary
    --version       version information

Exit status: 0 if anything matched, 1 if nothing did, 2 on an error.


mapsearch-config.xml
--------------------
mapsearch looks for a config, in order, at:

    the path given by --config=FILE
    the path in the MAPSEARCH_CONFIG environment variable
    ./mapsearch-config.xml
    mapsearch-config.xml next to the mapsearch binary

Without one, "." is searched recursively with the built-in flag property list
and every output option off. A commented sample sits next to this README. It
holds four things:

1) SearchIn -- directories to walk, each recursively. A single .map file also
   works.

2) Exclude -- paths to skip, each a regular expression that must match the
   whole thing. Each entry is tried against the full path with "/"
   separators, with "\" separators, and against the bare file or directory
   name, so a plain "autosave" skips TrenchBroom's autosave folders anywhere.

3) FlagsProperties -- properties whose value is a set of check boxes. An
   integer VALUE is bit-matched against these, and their values print with
   the bits spelled out. Listing the section replaces the built-in list
   rather than adding to it. The built-in list is spawnflags, flags, flags2,
   effects, items, items2, drawflags and artifact_flags; change it for
   another game.

4) The output options -- the same set as [OPTIONS] above, plus the log file
   name and a directory for .pts files. A letter on the command line beats
   what is here.

Element names are case-insensitive, and a value may be written any of four
ways:

    <SearchIn>path</SearchIn>            text in the section, one per line
    <SearchIn><Path>p</Path></SearchIn>  a leaf holding text; its name ignored
    <FlagsProperties><spawnflags/>       a leaf whose NAME is the value, with
                                         exclude="true" to park it
    <Output MakePts="true" />            settings written as attributes

A config written for Inky's build (his sample MapSearch1-config.xml) loads
here unchanged; copy it over as mapsearch-config.xml.


Coordinates
-----------
Every match prints the x y z of its entity, ready for TrenchBroom's
Menu > View > Camera > Move Camera to... box. For a point entity that is its
"origin". A brush entity has none, so the line is marked [brush] and shows the
first point of its first plane, which is near the brush but can fall outside
it.


Examples
--------
    mapsearch "*" spawnflags 64
Every entity with the 64 box checked in spawnflags (65 and 192 match too).

    mapsearch "*" "*" ".*\.wav"
Every property of every entity that names a wav file.

    mapsearch light_torch.* light "*"
Every torch variant and the light value it was given.

    mapsearch "*" spawnflags 2
Against the siege maps in gamecode/hc-unused:

    Line  15132:  (trigger_teleport)     spawnflags          == 131 [1][2][128]   at  -768 -1872 176  [brush]
    Line  21642:  (func_door_rotating)   opendraw.spawnflags == 98 [2][32][64]    at  -112 -264 -12   [brush]
    Line  19810:  (art_cubeofforce)      spawnflags          == 2 [2]             at  62 418 723

    mapsearch func_door.* classname
One line per func_door and func_door_rotating. "classname" always exists, so
naming it gives one line per entity rather than one per property.

    mapsearch func_door_rotating
No property named: every property of every match.

    mapsearch "*" "*" 128 +p
Anything set to 128, plus a .pts file per map. A spawnflags of 160 matches on
its 128 bit; a lip of 128 matches because it is exactly 128.
